  "summary_text": "Kyle, Port Washington is firing on all cylinders; Hicksville needs immediate attention on capacity and groomer productivity.\n\nPort Washington delivered 28% MTD growth over the prior July period, and critically, that's *real* performance\u2014DOW-adjusted expectation was +12.7%, so the team is outperforming calendar by 15 points. You've scaled groomer capacity from 3.8 to 4.7 per day month-over-month, and they're holding productivity near baseline ($495/groomer-day vs. $523 last month). Yesterday's 6-groomer day at $484/groomer was slightly soft, but that's noise; WTD you're up 7% on appointments, and YTD the store is +46% revenue and appointments versus last year. Retail is accelerating (+18% yesterday), which signals front-of-house momentum. One operational note: add-on attach rate ticked down from 54% to 50%\u2014small move, but worth a coaching conversation with the team on upsell discipline.\n\nHicksville is slipping. Grooming revenue fell 18% yesterday on 10 grooms (down from 13 last Thursday), which is pure capacity erosion; you're only at 2 groomer-days MTD versus 2.2 last month. At $396/groomer-day, the store is running 7% below its own baseline ($410), and productivity is trailing month-over-month. Retail spiked 160% yesterday\u2014genuine bright spot\u2014but that can't mask the core problem: fewer hands on the floor. Your lapse pool here is 119 customers worth $91K annually; that dormancy risk compounds if grooming capacity stays constrained. Add-on attach rate climbed to 54%, so upsell is healthy, but you can't upsell if appointments aren't flowing.\n\n**Capacity & coverage: Port Washington has 7 groomers + 7.0h bathers today and 4 groomers + 8.0h bathers tomorrow; Hicksville has 4 and 3 respectively; next 7 days Port is staffed at 34 groomer-days and Hicksville at 18, and that 1.9:1 ratio is widening your performance gap.**"
